There are several ways to horizontally center elements in CSS, depending on the type of element.

Usemargin: auto;, 
to horizontally center a block-level element (like <div>).

Also specify awidthfor the element, to prevent it from stretching out to the edges of its container.

Note:Center aligning has no effect on block-level elements if thewidthproperty is not set 
(or set to 100%).

Below, the <div> element is centered and has a width of 50% (and the remaining space 
will be split equally between the left and right margins):

To center the text inside a block-level element, usetext-align: center;.

Tip:For more examples on how to align text, see theCSS Textchapter.

To center an image, setmargin-leftandmargin-righttoauto, and 
also turn the image into ablockelement:

Another method for aligning elements is to useposition: absolute;:

Note:Absolute positioned elements are removed from the normal flow, and can overlap 
other elements.

Another method for aligning an element to the left or right, is to use thefloatproperty:

### Code Snippets

```
.center
{
margin: auto;
width: 50%;
border: 3px solid green;
padding: 10px;
}

---

p {
text-align: center;
}

---

img
{
display: block;
margin-left: auto;
margin-right: auto;
width: 40%;
}

---

.right
{
position: absolute;
right: 0px;
width: 300px;
border: 3px solid green;
padding: 10px;
}

---

.right
{
float: right;
width: 300px;
border: 3px solid green;
padding: 10px;
}
```
